<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>The Use of Large Language Models (LLMs)</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We disclose the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) in our work.</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Language polishing.</span> LLMs are used to improve grammar, style, and clarity of writing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Literature discovery.</span> LLMs are used as a retrieval and discovery tool to help identify potentially relevant prior works. All cited references, however, are carefully verified and selected by the authors.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The intellectual contributions, including problem formulation, theoretical analysis, algorithm design, and experimental work, are solely those of the authors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Additional Figures</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Validation of the Kernel’s Eigen-Decomposition on Unwhitened Data</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides additional figures for Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Empirical Validation of the Kernel’s Eigen-Decomposition ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, including the spectrum, relative error and squared weights analyzed on unwhitened data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/spectrum_Unwhitened.svg" id="A2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/relative_error_eigenvectors_Unwhitened.svg" id="A2.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A2.F1.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/weight_sq_Unwhitened.svg" id="A2.F1.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.1: </span>Empirical validation of the SSM-induced kernel on unwhitened data.
(a) Spectrum: empirical (blue dashed) vs. theoretical (orange crosses) eigenvalues on unwhitened data.
(b) Relative error between empirical and theoretical eigenvectors.
(c) Squared weights of empirical vs. theoretical eigenfunctions.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also provide the comparison of theoretical and empirical top eigenvectors. As shown in Figure <a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure B.1 ‣ B.1 Validation of the Kernel’s Eigen-Decomposition on Unwhitened Data ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A2.F2" title="Figure B.2 ‣ B.1 Validation of the Kernel’s Eigen-Decomposition on Unwhitened Data ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>, theory and experiment are in strong agreement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/eigenvector_comparison_whitened.svg" id="A2.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Whitened</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/eigenvector_comparison_Unwhitened.svg" id="A2.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Unwhitened</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.2: </span>Comparison of theoretical (orange crosses) and empirical (blue dashed) top eigenvectors.
(a) Top eigenvector of whitened data. (b) Top eigenvector of unwhitened data.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Learning Curves under More Parameter Settings</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides additional figures for Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Power Spectrum Matching Improves Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, including learning curves under more parameter settings, shown in Figure <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure B.3 ‣ B.2 Learning Curves under More Parameter Settings ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure B.4 ‣ B.2 Learning Curves under More Parameter Settings ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consistent with Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Power Spectrum Matching Improves Generalization ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, learning curves of the low-frequency task remain stable, while those of the high-frequency task evolve substantially during optimization. We also note that with noise <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the light blue curve exhibits larger errors under small-sample conditions (<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\sim 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∼</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\sim 100</annotation></semantics></math>) compared to the others (Figure <a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure B.4 ‣ B.2 Learning Curves under More Parameter Settings ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>). Because SSM parameters are tuned to high frequencies, the model confuses high-frequency signals with noise under small-sample conditions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/all_learning_curves_all.svg" id="A2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/322;" width="429" height="322"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.3: </span>Learning curves of low-frequency task under more parameter settings.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x6.svg" id="A2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/322;" width="429" height="322"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.4: </span>Learning curves of high-frequency task under more parameter settings.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct two SSMs, one with low-frequency bias and another with high-frequency bias (see Appendix <a href="#A4.SS4" title="D.4 Design of Low- and High-Frequency Biased SSMs ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.4</span></a> for construction details), and test them on both low- and high-frequency tasks. The results are shown in Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure B.5 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure B.6 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x7.svg" id="A2.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Power Spectrum</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x8.svg" id="A2.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Cumulative Power</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x9.svg" id="A2.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Generalization Error</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x10.svg" id="A2.F5.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Power Spectrum</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x11.svg" id="A2.F5.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(e)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Cumulative Power</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F5.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x12.svg" id="A2.F5.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F5.sf6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(f)</span> </span><span id="A2.F5.sf6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Generalization Error</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.5: </span>Comparison on low-frequency task. (a-c): Power spectrum, cumulative power curves and learning curves of low-frequency SSM. (d-f): Power spectrum, cumulative power curves and learning curves of high-frequency SSM. Colored lines denote different optimization iterations, while the black dashed line indicates the target spectrum.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x13.svg" id="A2.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Power Spectrum</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x14.svg" id="A2.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Cumulative Power</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x15.svg" id="A2.F6.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Generalization Error</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x16.svg" id="A2.F6.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Power Spectrum</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x17.svg" id="A2.F6.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(e)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Cumulative Power</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F6.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/x18.svg" id="A2.F6.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.sf6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(f)</span> </span><span id="A2.F6.sf6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Generalization Error</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.6: </span>Comparison on high-frequency task. (a-c): Power spectrum, cumulative power curves and learning curves of low-frequency SSM. (d-f): Power spectrum, cumulative power curves and learning curves of high-frequency SSM. Colored lines denote different optimization iterations, while the black dashed line indicates the target spectrum.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One key observation is that the two SSMs exhibit completely different properties. The initial power spectrum of low-frequency SSM is almost concentrated in the low-frequency range (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure B.5 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>(a), Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure B.6 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>(a)), while that of high-frequency SSM is the opposite (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure B.5 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>(d), Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure B.6 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>(d)). The distinct initial states steer optimization toward distinct outcomes. When the initial power spectrum poses a correct guess, SSM parameters are learned toward the right solution, resulting in faster-rising <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> and better generalization performance (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure B.5 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>(b-c), Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure B.6 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>(e-f), while a bad initialization results in an incorrect spectrum, slower-rising <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> and poorer generalization (Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure B.5 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>(e-f), Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure B.6 ‣ B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a>(b-c)). The results suggest that an appropriate initialization of the SSM parameters, and correspondingly an appropriate frequency bias, is crucial for good generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, it is observed that if the initialization of SSM parameters is suitable, the slight non-monotonic increase in generalization error under small samples can also be improved.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Power Spectrum of Some Datasets</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present additional figures for Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Application to Deep Learning Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. We demonstrate the power spectra of CIFAR-10, Pathfinder, ListOps, and Speech Commands. As shown in Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure B.7 ‣ B.4 Power Spectrum of Some Datasets ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.7</span></a>, Pathfinder and Speech Commands exhibit substantially richer high-frequency components compared to CIFAR-10. In contrast, ListOps is essentially a symbolic reasoning task and therefore lacks meaningful spectral structure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/cifar_spectrum.svg" id="A2.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">CIFAR-10</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/pathfinder_spectrum.svg" id="A2.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Pathfinder</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/listops_spectrum.svg" id="A2.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">ListOps</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A2.F7.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/sc_spectrum.svg" id="A2.F7.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F7.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A2.F7.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Speech Commands</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.7: </span>Power spectrum of real datasets. Pathfinder and Speech Commands exhibit richer high-frequency components than CIFAR-10, and richer spectral content across all frequencies than ListOps.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Training Curves of Deep SSMs</h3>

<div id="A2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we demonstrate representative training curves (test loss vs. epoch) for each dataset and training data ratio as supplementary figures for Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Application to Deep Learning Models ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, shown in Figure <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure B.8 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.8</span></a>, Figure <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure B.9 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a>, Figure <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure B.10 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.10</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure B.11 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.11</span></a>. Due to the inherent complexity of deep neural network training, we limit our discussion to high-level observations as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For tasks with rich frequency components, applying TDI generally leads to lower test loss in the small-data regime (Figures <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure B.9 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure B.10 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.10</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the dataset is sufficiently large (ratio close to 1), the TDI model may be slightly outperformed by the baseline model (Figures <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure B.9 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.9</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure B.10 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.10</span></a>). This instability in the large-data regime could be due to limiting power spectrum matching to a maximum of 20,000 training samples (see Appendix <a href="#A4.SS5" title="D.5 Methods and Hyperparameters Used in Deep SSM Training ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a>), or other factors such as suboptimal learning rate, which hinder effective training.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TDI appears to mitigate overfitting, as evidenced across all four datasets.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For tasks with poor frequency components, the difference between baseline and TDI remains minimal on small and large data ratios (Figures <a href="#A2.F8" title="Figure B.8 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.8</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F11" title="Figure B.11 ‣ B.5 Training Curves of Deep SSMs ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.11</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/cifar_test_loss_by_ratio_groups.svg" id="A2.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/215;" width="429" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.8: </span>Training curves on CIFAR-10</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/pathfind_test_loss_by_ratio_groups.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/215;" width="429" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.9: </span>Training curves on Pathfinder</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/sc_test_loss_by_ratio_groups.svg" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/215;" width="429" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.10: </span>Training curves on Speech Commands</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.20789v1/listops_test_loss_by_ratio_groups.svg" id="A2.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/215;" width="429" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure B.11: </span>Training curves on ListOps</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Supplementary Proofs</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section provides complete proofs of all the theorems and lemmas stated in the main text, with the exception of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 3.4. ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, whose more involved proof is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Relation Between Singular Values of Toeplitz Matrices and the Frequency Response of SSMs ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Characterizing SSM-Induced Kernel</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first present proofs characterizing the SSM-induced kernel. We begin by recalling our definition of SSM-induced kernel in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
See <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (SSM-Induced Kernel). ‣ 3.1 SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
We first show our kernel is valid by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Positive Semi-Definiteness). ‣ 3.1 SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Positive Semi-Definiteness). ‣ 3.1 SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Positive Semi-Definiteness). ‣ 3.1 SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By definition, for any set of input sequences <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{u}^{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{u}^{P}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>P</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{u}^{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{u}^{P}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Gram matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> has entries <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{\mu\nu}=K(\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu},\boldsymbol{u}^{\nu})=\frac{1}{L}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{{T_{K}}}\boldsymbol{u}^{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{\mu\nu}=K(\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu},\boldsymbol{u}^{\nu})=\frac{1}{L}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{{T_{K}}}\boldsymbol{u}^{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}=[\boldsymbol{u}^{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{u}^{P}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>P</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}=[\boldsymbol{u}^{1},\cdots,\boldsymbol{u}^{P}]</annotation></semantics></math> be the matrix of input sequences. The Gram matrix can be written in matrix form as <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}=\frac{1}{L}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}=\frac{1}{L}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U}).</annotation></semantics></math>
For any non-zero vector <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>P</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{z}^{\top}\mathbf{K}\boldsymbol{z}=\frac{1}{L}\boldsymbol{z}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})\boldsymbol{z}=\frac{1}{L}\left\lVert(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})\boldsymbol{z}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐔</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒛</mi></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{z}^{\top}\mathbf{K}\boldsymbol{z}=\frac{1}{L}\boldsymbol{z}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})^{\top}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})\boldsymbol{z}=\frac{1}{L}\left\lVert(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{U})\boldsymbol{z}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, since the Gram matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive semi-definite (and symmetric obviously), the kernel <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is valid.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Inserting the singular value decomposition (SVD) of Toeplitz matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T_{K}}=\sqrt{L}\boldsymbol{USV}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>L</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T_{K}}=\sqrt{L}\boldsymbol{USV}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> into Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (SSM-Induced Kernel). ‣ 3.1 SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we obtain <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime})=(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u})^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{2}(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑺</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime})=(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u})^{\top}\boldsymbol{S}^{2}(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we prove rows of <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u}</annotation></semantics></math> form a set of eigenfunctions of the kernel under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2 (Orthonormal Eigenfunctions). ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2 (Orthonormal Eigenfunctions). ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{v}^{\top}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{v}^{\top}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th row of the right singular matrix <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. For any integer <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[1,L]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[1,L]</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int(\boldsymbol{v}^{\top}_{i}\boldsymbol{u})\cdot(\boldsymbol{v}^{\top}_{j}\boldsymbol{u})p(\boldsymbol{u})d\boldsymbol{u}=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}\left[\left(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{uu}^{\top}\boldsymbol{V}\right)_{ij}\right]=\left(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{u}\boldsymbol{u}^{\top}]\boldsymbol{V}\right)_{ij}=\delta_{ij}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int(\boldsymbol{v}^{\top}_{i}\boldsymbol{u})\cdot(\boldsymbol{v}^{\top}_{j}\boldsymbol{u})p(\boldsymbol{u})d\boldsymbol{u}=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}\left[\left(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{uu}^{\top}\boldsymbol{V}\right)_{ij}\right]=\left(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{u}\boldsymbol{u}^{\top}]\boldsymbol{V}\right)_{ij}=\delta_{ij}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">The last step follows from Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1. ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and the fact that the columns of <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthonormal vectors.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1 (Positive Semi-Definiteness). ‣ 3.1 SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2 (Orthonormal Eigenfunctions). ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and Mercer’s Theorem (Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1 (Mercer’s Theorem ( , )). ‣ 2.3 A Kernel-Theoretic Framework for Analyzing Inductive Bias ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), we derive Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (Eigen-Decomposition of SSM-Induced Kernel). ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.
See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (Eigen-Decomposition of SSM-Induced Kernel). ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Power Spectrum Matching Improves Generalization</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we present proofs showing that minimizing power spectrum matching loss <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> improves generalization by promoting a faster rise in cumulative power <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert H(\omega)\rvert^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert H(\omega)\rvert^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the model power spectrum derived from the SSM’s intrinsic frequency response and <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the task power spectrum estimated from the Fourier transform of the input-output cross-variance matrix <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{uy}^{\top}]\approx\frac{1}{P}\sum_{\mu=1}^{P}\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu}{\boldsymbol{y}^{\mu}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>P</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow></mrow><mi>μ</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{uy}^{\top}]\approx\frac{1}{P}\sum_{\mu=1}^{P}\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu}{\boldsymbol{y}^{\mu}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. The power spectrum matching loss is defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.
See <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (Power Spectrum Matching Loss). ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
We provide a two-part theoretical justification. First, we show that maximizing a general alignment objective <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, promotes a rapid increase in <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>). Then we show that minimizing power spectrum matching loss <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an efficient strategy for maximizing <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>).
See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since the eigenfunctions of SSM-induced kernel are linear for any input data <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\rho}(\boldsymbol{u})=\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\boldsymbol{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\rho}(\boldsymbol{u})=\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\boldsymbol{u}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear operator. Let <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{w}_{\rho,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{w}_{\rho,j}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the projection coefficient of the <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th component of <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{w}_{\rho,j}=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\phi_{\rho}(\boldsymbol{u})\boldsymbol{y}_{j}]=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\boldsymbol{u}\cdot\boldsymbol{y}_{j}]=\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{u}\cdot\boldsymbol{y}_{j}]=\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\mathbf{C}_{j}=\phi_{\rho}(\mathbf{C}_{j})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>𝒖</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝜶</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{w}_{\rho,j}=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\phi_{\rho}(\boldsymbol{u})\boldsymbol{y}_{j}]=\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\boldsymbol{u}\cdot\boldsymbol{y}_{j}]=\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\mathbb{E}_{\boldsymbol{u}}[\boldsymbol{u}\cdot\boldsymbol{y}_{j}]=\boldsymbol{\alpha}_{\rho}\mathbf{C}_{j}=\phi_{\rho}(\mathbf{C}_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th column of <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\equiv\frac{1}{L}\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}\rVert_{F}^{2}=\sum_{\rho=1}^{L}\eta_{\rho}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\rho}(\mathbf{C}_{j})^{2}=\sum_{\rho=1}^{L}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\eta_{\rho}\overline{w}_{\rho,j}^{2}=\sum_{\rho=1}^{L}\eta_{\rho}\overline{w}_{\rho}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\equiv\frac{1}{L}\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}\rVert_{F}^{2}=\sum_{\rho=1}^{L}\eta_{\rho}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\phi_{\rho}(\mathbf{C}_{j})^{2}=\sum_{\rho=1}^{L}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\eta_{\rho}\overline{w}_{\rho,j}^{2}=\sum_{\rho=1}^{L}\eta_{\rho}\overline{w}_{\rho}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">The last step follows the definition in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Preliminaries ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
During optimization, both the eigenvalues <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{w}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{w}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> are functions of the SSM parameters being tuned. The eigenvalues are, by definition, sorted in descending order. By the rearrangement inequality, the sum of products of two sequences is maximized when both sequences are sorted in the same order. To maximize <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimization process is therefore incentivized to find parameters that sort the sequence of <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\overline{w}_{\rho}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\overline{w}_{\rho}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the same descending order as the eigenvalues, which is precisely the condition for a rapidly increasing cumulative power curve. Thus, maximizing <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> promotes a faster-rising <math id="A3.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Expanding the general alignment objective <math id="A3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\frac{1}{L}\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}\rVert_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\frac{1}{L}\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}\rVert_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as the sum of squared Euclidean norms, <math id="A3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=\frac{1}{L}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{j}\rVert_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=\frac{1}{L}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{j}\rVert_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (recall that <math id="A3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimension of labels). By Parseval’s Theorem, the norm of a vector is proportional to the norm of its Fourier transform. Thus,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{j}\rVert_{2}^{2}\propto\sum_{\omega}\lvert\mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{j})(\omega)\rvert^{2}=\sum_{\omega}\left\lvert\mathcal{F}(\overline{\boldsymbol{k}}*\mathbf{C}_{j})\right\rvert^{2}=\sum_{\omega}\lvert H(\omega)\rvert^{2}\lvert G_{\mathbf{C}_{j}}(\omega)\rvert^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">∝</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>ω</mi></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>𝑲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>ω</mi></munder><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒌</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>ω</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{j}\rVert_{2}^{2}\propto\sum_{\omega}\lvert\mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\mathbf{C}_{j})(\omega)\rvert^{2}=\sum_{\omega}\left\lvert\mathcal{F}(\overline{\boldsymbol{k}}*\mathbf{C}_{j})\right\rvert^{2}=\sum_{\omega}\lvert H(\omega)\rvert^{2}\lvert G_{\mathbf{C}_{j}}(\omega)\rvert^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the last step follows from Convolution Theorem. Substituting this back into the expression for <math id="A3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>, we arrive at the frequency-domain representation,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\propto\sum_{\omega}\lvert H(\omega)\rvert^{2}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\lvert G_{\mathbf{C}_{j}}(\omega)\rvert^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∝</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>ω</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\propto\sum_{\omega}\lvert H(\omega)\rvert^{2}\sum_{j=1}^{d}\lvert G_{\mathbf{C}_{j}}(\omega)\rvert^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">This equation shows that <math id="A3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is proportional to the inner product between the model’s power spectrum and the task’s total power spectrum. By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, this inner product is maximized when the two spectra are maximally aligned. Therefore, minimizing <math id="A3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> is an equivalent and efficient method for maximizing the general alignment objective <math id="A3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, we derive Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Power Spectrum Matching Improves Generalization Ability). ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>.
See <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Power Spectrum Matching Improves Generalization Ability). ‣ 3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide some details of our experiments.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Diagonalizing Kernel on Real Datasets</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Empirical eigenvalues and eigenfunctions are obtained by diagonalizing the Gram matrix of the whole dataset (trainset <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> testset), <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}_{\mu\nu}=K(\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu},\boldsymbol{u}^{\nu})\,,\mu,\nu=1,\cdots,M," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐊</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}_{\mu\nu}=K(\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu},\boldsymbol{u}^{\nu})\,,\mu,\nu=1,\cdots,M,</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;P</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the size of the whole dataset. Diagonalizing <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}=M\mathbf{\Phi\Lambda\Phi}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}=M\mathbf{\Phi\Lambda\Phi}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the diagonal entries of <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> are the empirical eigenvalues and the columns of the orthogonal matrix <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are the empirical eigenvectors.
For these empirical eigenfunctions to be orthonormal in the RKHS, they must satisfy the orthonormality condition with respect to the empirical data distribution <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\boldsymbol{u})=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\mu=1}^{M}\delta(\boldsymbol{u}-\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒖</mi><mi>μ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\boldsymbol{u})=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\mu=1}^{M}\delta(\boldsymbol{u}-\boldsymbol{u}^{\mu})</annotation></semantics></math>. The empirical eigenfunctions are given by <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚽</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, because</p>
<table id="A4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\int\left(\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{:,i}\right)\cdot\left(\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{:,j}\right)p(\boldsymbol{u})d\boldsymbol{u}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\mu=1}^{M}\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{\mu i}\cdot\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{\mu j}=\delta_{ij}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><msub><mi>𝚽</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\int\left(\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{:,i}\right)\cdot\left(\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{:,j}\right)p(\boldsymbol{u})d\boldsymbol{u}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{\mu=1}^{M}\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{\mu i}\cdot\sqrt{M}\mathbf{\Phi}_{\mu j}=\delta_{ij}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The last step follows from the fact that <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthogonal matrix. Thus, the empirical eigenvalues and eigenfunctions derived from the Gram matrix serve as empirical counterparts to the theoretical ones from Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (Eigen-Decomposition of SSM-Induced Kernel). ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3.3 (Eigen-Decomposition of SSM-Induced Kernel). ‣ 3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> states that the SSM-induced kernel has a rank of at most <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of non-zero empirical eigenvalues typically exceeds <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> due to numerical noise. However, the spectrum of <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> rapidly decays after <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;L</annotation></semantics></math>, and the learned weights in those directions are nearly zero (see Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Empirical Validation of the Kernel’s Eigen-Decomposition ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>). This finding corroborates our theory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Synthetic Task Generation</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the synthetic low- and high-frequency tasks, we generate labels based on MNIST images as follows. Let <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{P\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{P\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mean-centered MNIST data matrix, with each image reshaped into a vector. We define a temporal grid <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,1]^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,1]^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and construct frequency patterns corresponding to low- or high-frequency components,</p>
<table id="A4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\text{low}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\text{low}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\cos(2\pi\cdot 20t)+\cos(2\pi\cdot 40t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\cos(2\pi\cdot 20t)+\cos(2\pi\cdot 40t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\text{high}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\text{high}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\cos(2\pi\cdot 300t)+\sin(2\pi\cdot 350t)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>350</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\cos(2\pi\cdot 300t)+\sin(2\pi\cdot 350t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which are <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-normalized and used to generate labels as <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{U}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}=\mathbf{U}p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This procedure ensures that the synthetic labels inherit the desired frequency structure while preserving the spatial correlations of MNIST.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Optimization of the SSM Parameters</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To fit the SSM to a given task, we parameterize it as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. [2022b]</a></cite>
<math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}=-\exp(\log(-\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}))+i\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}=-\exp(\log(-\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}))+i\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, the output matrix <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and time step <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\exp(\log dt)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\exp(\log dt)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(-\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}),\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A},\boldsymbol{C},\log dt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(-\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}),\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A},\boldsymbol{C},\log dt</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable parameters. For multi-dimensional outputs, we first compute the cross-covariance matrix <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}=\mathbf{U}^{\top}\mathbf{Y}/P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐘</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}}=\mathbf{U}^{\top}\mathbf{Y}/P</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding power spectrum <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}})\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}})\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> averaged across the label dimension <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The SSM parameters are optimized by minimizing the power spectrum matching loss using Adam with a cosine-annealing scheduler. The details are implemented in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ D.3 Optimization of the SSM Parameters ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Optimizing S4D Parameter via Power Spectrum Matching</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span> <span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FindS4D</span>(<math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\boldsymbol{uy}},L,N,\theta_{\text{init}},\eta,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\boldsymbol{uy}},L,N,\theta_{\text{init}},\eta,T</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Task power spectrum <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}</annotation></semantics></math>, seq length <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, state size <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, initial parameters <math id="alg1.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate <math id="alg1.l2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, iterations <math id="alg1.l2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Optimized SSM parameters <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}=\{\boldsymbol{A}^{*},\boldsymbol{C}^{*},{\Delta}^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}=\{\boldsymbol{A}^{*},\boldsymbol{C}^{*},{\Delta}^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              Initialize trainable parameters <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\log\boldsymbol{A}_{\text{real}},\boldsymbol{A}_{\text{imag}},\boldsymbol{C},\log{\Delta}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>real</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>imag</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\log\boldsymbol{A}_{\text{real}},\boldsymbol{A}_{\text{imag}},\boldsymbol{C},\log{\Delta}\}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Initialize Adam optimizer and Cosine Annealing scheduler.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               Reconstruct <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math> from trainable parameters.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\boldsymbol{t}}\leftarrow\text{ComputeS4DImpulseResponse}(\boldsymbol{A},\boldsymbol{C},{\Delta},L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>ComputeS4DImpulseResponse</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\boldsymbol{t}}\leftarrow\text{ComputeS4DImpulseResponse}(\boldsymbol{A},\boldsymbol{C},{\Delta},L)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{ssm}}\leftarrow\text{Normalize}(|\text{FFT}(\bar{\boldsymbol{t}})|^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>ssm</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Normalize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mtext>FFT</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{ssm}}\leftarrow\text{Normalize}(|\text{FFT}(\bar{\boldsymbol{t}})|^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{task}}\leftarrow\text{Normalize}(|G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Normalize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{task}}\leftarrow\text{Normalize}(|G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}|)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L_{F}}\leftarrow\text{MSE}(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{ssm}},\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{task}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>MSE</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>ssm</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐒</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>task</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L_{F}}\leftarrow\text{MSE}(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{ssm}},\hat{\mathbf{S}}_{\text{task}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
               Update trainable parameters using gradient descent on <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
              Reconstruct final parameters <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}^{*},\boldsymbol{C}^{*},{\Delta}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}^{*},\boldsymbol{C}^{*},{\Delta}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{A}^{*},\boldsymbol{C}^{*},{\Delta}^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{A}^{*},\boldsymbol{C}^{*},{\Delta}^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Design of Low- and High-Frequency Biased SSMs</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiment, the parameters of the low-frequency biased SSM are set by</p>
<table id="A4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\frac{0.5}{N},\quad\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}=\pi\cdot\{0,1,\cdots,\lfloor n/2\rfloor\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>0.5</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\frac{0.5}{N},\quad\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}=\pi\cdot\{0,1,\cdots,\lfloor n/2\rfloor\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and those of the high-frequency biased SSM are</p>
<table id="A4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\frac{1}{N},\quad\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}=\pi\cdot\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\cdots,n-1\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\frac{1}{N},\quad\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}=\pi\cdot\{\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\cdots,n-1\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="A4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\boldsymbol{I}),\quad\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}=\{-0.5,-0.5,\cdots,-0.5\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\boldsymbol{I}),\quad\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}=\{-0.5,-0.5,\cdots,-0.5\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">are fixed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.5 </span>Methods and Hyperparameters Used in Deep SSM Training</h3>

<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both the baseline and TDI models employ the same hyperparameters, with the only distinction being that the first S4 layer of the TDI model is optimized via spectrum matching (see Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 Optimization of the SSM Parameters ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> and Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ D.5 Methods and Hyperparameters Used in Deep SSM Training ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) by a maximum of 20,000 training samples, i.e., <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min(\text{train samples, 20000)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>train samples, 20000)</mtext><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min(\text{train samples, 20000)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, the imaginary part of <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}</annotation></semantics></math> in the first layer of the TDI model is initialized by Theorem <a href="#A6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem F.1. ‣ Appendix F Further Analysis of SSM’s Frequency Bias ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>, where the frequencies <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the top 64 values of <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}(\omega)\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}(\omega)\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, given by <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}})\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{C}_{\boldsymbol{uy}})\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> averaged across the label dimension <math id="A4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. All other layers are initialized using S4D-Lin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2022b</a>]</cite>. Each model contains four S4 layers with a state size of 64 and feature dimension of 256. Other hyperparameters are reported in Table <a href="#A4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ D.5 Methods and Hyperparameters Used in Deep SSM Training ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Main Loop for Optimizing the First Layer of Multi-Head Deep SSM Model</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Heads <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, initial parameters <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{\text{init}},\mathbf{C}_{\text{init}},\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{init}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{\text{init}},\mathbf{C}_{\text{init}},\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{init}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, task power spectrum <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\boldsymbol{uy}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Final multi-head parameters <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{\text{final}},\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}},\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{final}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{\text{final}},\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}},\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{final}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Initialize empty parameter lists <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{\text{list}},\mathcal{C}_{\text{list}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{list}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>list</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>list</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>list</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{\text{list}},\mathcal{C}_{\text{list}},\mathcal{D}_{\text{list}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Get initial parameters <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{init}}^{(h)}=\{\boldsymbol{A}_{\text{init}}^{(h)},\boldsymbol{C}_{\text{init}}^{(h)},\Delta_{\text{init}}^{(h)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑪</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{init}}^{(h)}=\{\boldsymbol{A}_{\text{init}}^{(h)},\boldsymbol{C}_{\text{init}}^{(h)},\Delta_{\text{init}}^{(h)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for head <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> by slicing inputs.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*(h)}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*(h)}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FindS4D</span>(<math id="alg2.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\boldsymbol{uy}},L,N,\theta_{\text{init}}^{(h)},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>init</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\boldsymbol{uy}},L,N,\theta_{\text{init}}^{(h)},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Append learned parameters <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{A}^{*(h)},\boldsymbol{C}^{*(h)},{\Delta}^{*(h)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{A}^{*(h)},\boldsymbol{C}^{*(h)},{\Delta}^{*(h)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to lists.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow\text{Concatenate and stabilize}(\mathcal{A}_{\text{list}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Concatenate and stabilize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mtext>list</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow\text{Concatenate and stabilize}(\mathcal{A}_{\text{list}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow\text{Concatenate}(\mathcal{C}_{\text{list}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Concatenate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mtext>list</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow\text{Concatenate}(\mathcal{C}_{\text{list}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow\text{Concatenate}(\mathcal{D}_{\text{list}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Concatenate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>list</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow\text{Concatenate}(\mathcal{D}_{\text{list}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg2.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{\text{init}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>init</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{\text{init}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a channel dimension <span id="alg2.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
              Expand <math id="alg2.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}}</annotation></semantics></math> to match channel dimensions.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{A}_{\text{final}},\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}},\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{final}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝚫</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{A}_{\text{final}},\mathbf{C}_{\text{final}},\boldsymbol{\Delta}_{\text{final}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
