<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Relation Between Singular Values of Toeplitz Matrices and the Frequency Response of SSMs</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze the relation between singular values of the SSM’s Toeplitz and the frequency response of SSM. We begin by introducing a lemma from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gray et al. [2006]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma E.1</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lemma 4.6 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gray et al. [2006]</a></cite>)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}_{n}(f)=\{t_{k-j}\}_{j,k=0,\cdots,n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}_{n}(f)=\{t_{k-j}\}_{j,k=0,\cdots,n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> where</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\lvert t_{k}\rvert&lt;\infty," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\lvert t_{k}\rvert&lt;\infty,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\lambda)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{k}e^{ik\lambda},\quad\hat{f}_{n}(\lambda)=\sum_{k=-(n-1)}^{n-1}t_{k}e^{ik\lambda}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\lambda)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{k}e^{ik\lambda},\quad\hat{f}_{n}(\lambda)=\sum_{k=-(n-1)}^{n-1}t_{k}e^{ik\lambda}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define the circulant matrices <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> with top row <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{0}^{(n)},\cdots,c_{n-1}^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{0}^{(n)},\cdots,c_{n-1}^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(\hat{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(\hat{f})</annotation></semantics></math> with top row <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{c}_{0}^{(n)},\cdots,\hat{c}_{n-1}^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{c}_{0}^{(n)},\cdots,\hat{c}_{n-1}^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math> where</span></p>
<table id="A5.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{k}^{(n)}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}f(2\pi j/n)e^{2\pi ijk/n},\quad\hat{c}_{k}^{(n)}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\hat{f}_{n}(2\pi j/n)e^{2\pi ijk/n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{k}^{(n)}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}f(2\pi j/n)e^{2\pi ijk/n},\quad\hat{c}_{k}^{(n)}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\hat{f}_{n}(2\pi j/n)e^{2\pi ijk/n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f)\sim\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(\hat{f}_{n})\sim\boldsymbol{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f)\sim\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(\hat{f}_{n})\sim\boldsymbol{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma E.1 (Lemma 4.6 of [ ] ). ‣ Appendix E Relation Between Singular Values of Toeplitz Matrices and the Frequency Response of SSMs ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a> states that as <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is asymptotically equivalent to a circulant matrix <math id="A5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(\hat{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(\hat{f})</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on the fact that the eigenvalues of a circulant matrix are given by the discrete Fourier transform of its top row <math id="A5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c^{(n)}_{0},\cdots,c_{n-1}^{(n)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c^{(n)}_{0},\cdots,c_{n-1}^{(n)})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{k}=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}c_{j}^{(n)}e^{-2\pi ijk/n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{k}=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}c_{j}^{(n)}e^{-2\pi ijk/n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">we obtain</p>
<table id="A5.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{k}(\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f))=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{l=0}^{n-1}f(2\pi l/n)e^{2\pi ijl/n}e^{-2\pi ijk/n}=f(2\pi k/n)=\sum_{p=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{p}e^{ip\cdot 2\pi k/n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{k}(\boldsymbol{C}_{n}(f))=\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{l=0}^{n-1}f(2\pi l/n)e^{2\pi ijl/n}e^{-2\pi ijk/n}=f(2\pi k/n)=\sum_{p=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{p}e^{ip\cdot 2\pi k/n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is circulant, its singular values <math id="A5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are equal to the absolute value of its eigenvalues <math id="A5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus we arrive at</p>
<table id="A5.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{k}(\boldsymbol{T}_{n})\sim s_{k}(\boldsymbol{C}_{n})=\lvert\lambda_{k}(\boldsymbol{C}_{n})\rvert=\left\lvert\sum_{p=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{p}e^{ip\cdot 2\pi k/n}\right\rvert." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑻</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{k}(\boldsymbol{T}_{n})\sim s_{k}(\boldsymbol{C}_{n})=\lvert\lambda_{k}(\boldsymbol{C}_{n})\rvert=\left\lvert\sum_{p=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{p}e^{ip\cdot 2\pi k/n}\right\rvert.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.4" class="ltx_p">For the SSM’s Toeplitz matrix, <math id="A5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{p}=\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{p}=\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{-p}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 State Space Model and the Related Toeplitz Matrix as Linear Operators ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). Then, <math id="A5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{p&gt;0}=0,t_{p\leq 0}=\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{p&gt;0}=0,t_{p\leq 0}=\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{-p}</annotation></semantics></math> is the impulse response kernel of SSM. Then,</p>
<table id="A5.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{k}=\left\lvert\sum_{p=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{p}e^{ip\cdot 2\pi k/n}\right\rvert=\left\lvert\sum_{p=0}^{\infty}\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{p}e^{-ip\omega_{k}}\right\rvert=\lvert H(\omega_{k})\rvert," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{k}=\left\lvert\sum_{p=-\infty}^{\infty}t_{p}e^{ip\cdot 2\pi k/n}\right\rvert=\left\lvert\sum_{p=0}^{\infty}\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{p}e^{-ip\omega_{k}}\right\rvert=\lvert H(\omega_{k})\rvert,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.p2.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=\frac{2\pi k}{n}(k=0,\cdots,n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=\frac{2\pi k}{n}(k=0,\cdots,n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly the frequency response (or transfer function) of the SSM (see Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 State Space Model and the Related Toeplitz Matrix as Linear Operators ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). We summarize this result in Theorem <a href="#A5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem E.2 (Asymptotic Behavior of Singular Values). ‣ Appendix E Relation Between Singular Values of Toeplitz Matrices and the Frequency Response of SSMs ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.2</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic Behavior of Singular Values)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For large <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th singular value <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of the SSM’s Toeplitz matrix <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐊</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the frequency response <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert H(\omega_{k})\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert H(\omega_{k})\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=\frac{2\pi k}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=\frac{2\pi k}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,\dots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,\dots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the derivation of singular values, we can analogously show that the <math id="A5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of <math id="A5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the Fourier basis with frequency <math id="A5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem E.3</span></span><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Asymptotic Behavior of Right Singular Vectors)<span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For large <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th row of the right singular matrix <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> of the SSM’s Toeplitz matrix <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐓</mi><mi>𝐊</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{T_{K}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the Fourier basis with frequency <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0,\dots,n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0,\dots,n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that the singular values and the right singular matrix are not sorted here; the index <math id="A5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the corresponding frequency <math id="A5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=\frac{2\pi k}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=\frac{2\pi k}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the unwhitened data case, <math id="A5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Characterizing the SSM-Induced Kernel ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> can be expressed as <math id="A5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}=\boldsymbol{USV}^{\top}\boldsymbol{QDQ}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}=\boldsymbol{USV}^{\top}\boldsymbol{QDQ}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{QDQ}^{\top}=\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{\frac{1}{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑸</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝚺</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{QDQ}^{\top}=\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{\frac{1}{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the SVD of <math id="A5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the SVD of <math id="A5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}=\boldsymbol{S}(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Q})\boldsymbol{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑺</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}=\boldsymbol{S}(\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Q})\boldsymbol{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}=\boldsymbol{UU_{M}\Sigma_{M}V_{M}}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Q}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>𝑴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>𝑴</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑸</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}=\boldsymbol{UU_{M}\Sigma_{M}V_{M}}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Q}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}=\boldsymbol{U_{M}\Sigma_{M}V_{M}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑼</mi><mi>𝑴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>𝑴</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝑽</mi><mi>𝑴</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}=\boldsymbol{U_{M}\Sigma_{M}V_{M}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since both <math id="A5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> are orthogonal, <math id="A5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑸</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}\boldsymbol{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is itself an orthogonal change of basis: it maps the Fourier basis <math id="A5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> into the eigenbasis of the data covariance (columns of <math id="A5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑸</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Therefore, the singular values of <math id="A5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence of <math id="A5.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\boldsymbol{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, are governed by how the frequency response encoded in <math id="A5.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{S}</annotation></semantics></math> aligns with the spectral distribution of the high-variance directions encoded in <math id="A5.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, a large singular value arises when a dominant data mode (large <math id="A5.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{D}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑫</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{D}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) has most of its energy concentrated in frequencies where the LTI operator exhibits strong gain, resulting in faster learning at the corresponding basis. Actually, our power spectrum matching method in Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Designing SSM Parameter via Task-Dependent Initialization ‣ 3 Methods ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> is inspired by this finding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Further Analysis of SSM’s Frequency Bias</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will analyze the relation between frequency bias of an SSM and its parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, we restate the definition of frequency bias in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. [2024]</a></cite>: frequency bias of an SSM means that the frequency responses of the systems have more variation in the low-frequency area than the high-frequency area.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The frequency response <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\omega)</annotation></semantics></math> of an SSM is actually the discrete Fourier
transform of its impulse response <math id="A6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A6.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\omega)=\operatorname{DFT}(\overline{\boldsymbol{t}})=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{n}e^{-i\omega n},\quad\omega\in(0,2\pi)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>DFT</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\omega)=\operatorname{DFT}(\overline{\boldsymbol{t}})=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{n}e^{-i\omega n},\quad\omega\in(0,2\pi).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Substituting <math id="A6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{n}=\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}\overline{\boldsymbol{A}}^{n}\overline{\boldsymbol{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒕</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\boldsymbol{t}}_{n}=\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}\overline{\boldsymbol{A}}^{n}\overline{\boldsymbol{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A6.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(\omega)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}\overline{\boldsymbol{A}}^{n}\overline{\boldsymbol{B}}e^{-i\omega n}=\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}(\boldsymbol{I}-\overline{\boldsymbol{A}}e^{-i\omega})^{-1}\overline{\boldsymbol{B}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(\omega)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}\overline{\boldsymbol{A}}^{n}\overline{\boldsymbol{B}}e^{-i\omega n}=\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}(\boldsymbol{I}-\overline{\boldsymbol{A}}e^{-i\omega})^{-1}\overline{\boldsymbol{B}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We substitute <math id="A6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A},\boldsymbol{B},\boldsymbol{C},\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝑪</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A},\boldsymbol{B},\boldsymbol{C},\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="A6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\boldsymbol{A}},\overline{\boldsymbol{B}},\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑨</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑩</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\boldsymbol{A}},\overline{\boldsymbol{B}},\overline{\boldsymbol{C}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="A6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{B}=\boldsymbol{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{B}=\boldsymbol{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> (fixed <math id="A6.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{B}</annotation></semantics></math> in S4D training), we derive</p>
<table id="A6.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(\omega)=\sum_{k}\boldsymbol{C}_{k}\left(e^{\Delta\boldsymbol{A}_{k}}-1\right)\frac{1}{\boldsymbol{A}_{k}}\frac{1}{1-e^{-i\omega+\Delta\boldsymbol{A}_{k}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(\omega)=\sum_{k}\boldsymbol{C}_{k}\left(e^{\Delta\boldsymbol{A}_{k}}-1\right)\frac{1}{\boldsymbol{A}_{k}}\frac{1}{1-e^{-i\omega+\Delta\boldsymbol{A}_{k}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A6.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th diagonal element of <math id="A6.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A6.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th element of <math id="A6.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. This result is similar to equation (3) in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. [2024]</a></cite>.
<math id="A6.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert H(\omega)\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert H(\omega)\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> attains an extremum when the denominator reaches its extremum, which is given by</p>
<table id="A6.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d}{d\omega}\operatorname{Re}e^{-i\omega+\Delta(\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}+i\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k})}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d}{d\omega}\operatorname{Re}e^{-i\omega+\Delta(\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}+i\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k})}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p3.5" class="ltx_p">Thus, <math id="A6.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=\Delta\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=\Delta\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the location of the extremum of <math id="A6.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert H(\omega)\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert H(\omega)\rvert</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, the corresponding <math id="A6.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑪</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> determine the magnitude at <math id="A6.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=\Delta\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=\Delta\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem F.1</span></span><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lvert H(\omega)\rvert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lvert H(\omega)\rvert</annotation></semantics></math> be the frequency response of an S4D-like SSM with parameters <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}\in\mathbb{C}^{n\times n},\boldsymbol{C}\in\mathbb{C}^{1\times n},\Delta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐂</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}\in\mathbb{C}^{n\times n},\boldsymbol{C}\in\mathbb{C}^{1\times n},\Delta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the location of its extremum is determined by <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=\Delta\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=\Delta\operatorname{Im}\boldsymbol{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the magnitude of the extremum is determined by <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Re</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Re}\boldsymbol{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This result can successfully explain the frequency bias of the SSM. We now provide an estimate for S4D. Since the original S4D initialization gives</p>
<table id="A6.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}_{k}=-0.5+i\pi\cdot k,\quad k=1,\cdots,\lfloor n/2\rfloor," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}_{k}=-0.5+i\pi\cdot k,\quad k=1,\cdots,\lfloor n/2\rfloor,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">the extremum points are then determined by <math id="A6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}=\pi\Delta k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}=\pi\Delta k</annotation></semantics></math>. Typically, the number of states, <math id="A6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, is 64, and <math id="A6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from a log-uniform distribution between 0.001 and 0.1. Thus, the proportion of the largest <math id="A6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to <math id="A6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{n}/\pi=\Delta\cdot\lfloor n/2\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{n}/\pi=\Delta\cdot\lfloor n/2\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies a log-uniform distribution between 0.032 and 3.2. Thus, <math id="A6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\omega_{n}/\pi&lt;0.1)=0.25,P(\omega_{n}/\pi&lt;0.3)=0.49,P(\omega_{n}/\pi&lt;0.6)=0.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.64</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\omega_{n}/\pi&lt;0.1)=0.25,P(\omega_{n}/\pi&lt;0.3)=0.49,P(\omega_{n}/\pi&lt;0.6)=0.64</annotation></semantics></math>. This result shows that with the initialization of S4D, the informative frequency response, i.e., frequency area with more variation, has a high probability to concentrate in a narrow, low-frequency area, causing a low frequency bias of SSM. The first approach to tuning the frequency bias in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. [2024]</a></cite>, involving the simultaneous multiplication of all frequencies by a coefficient, is equivalent to adjusting the range of <math id="A6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#A6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem F.1. ‣ Appendix F Further Analysis of SSM’s Frequency Bias ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a> also provides an instruction on SSM parameter initialization. With inappropriate initialization, double descent of minimizing the power spectrum matching loss <math id="A6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> may lead to local minima (see Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 SSMs with Low- and High-Frequency Bias ‣ Appendix B Additional Figures ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>). Thus, it is crucial to design an initialization that guides the optimizer toward the correct solution. For example, the imaginary part of <math id="A6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{A}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{A}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> can be initialized according to Theorem <a href="#A6.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem F.1. ‣ Appendix F Further Analysis of SSM’s Frequency Bias ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>, as detailed in Appendix <a href="#A4.SS5" title="D.5 Methods and Hyperparameters Used in Deep SSM Training ‣ Appendix D Experimental Details ‣ Aligning Inductive Bias for Data-Efficient Generalization in State Space Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
